 "context": "<figure id=\"fig-ch10_04_01\" class=\"  \"><span data-type=\"media\" id=\"fs-id847150\" data-alt=\"Part a: This illustration shows cell cycle regulation by normal p53, which arrests the cell cycle in response to DNA damage, cell cycle abnormalities, or hypoxia. Once the damage is repaired, the cell cycle restarts. If the damage cannot be repaired, apoptosis (programmed cell death) occurs. Part b: Mutated p53 does not arrest the cell cycle in response to cellular damage. As a result, the cell cycle continues, and the cell may become cancerous.\">\n<img src=\"https://openstax.org/apps/archive/20240910.161227/resources/29877888d2d29db4236ad6477bd15dcaf6fc74d7\" data-media-type=\"image/png\" alt=\"Part a: This illustration shows cell cycle regulation by normal p53, which arrests the cell cycle in response to DNA damage, cell cycle abnormalities, or hypoxia. Once the damage is repaired, the cell cycle restarts. If the damage cannot be repaired, apoptosis (programmed cell death) occurs. Part b: Mutated p53 does not arrest the cell cycle in response to cellular damage. As a result, the cell cycle continues, and the cell may become cancerous.\" width=\"500\" height=\"412\">\n</span>\n</figure>",
 "stimulus_html": "",
